  "exact_strip_carrier": {
    "base": "Part3.labelledPrimeTuples (s.1.val+(2+s.2.val)) (2*N)",
    "predicates": [
      "original admissibleSplit s",
      "w = original tuplePrefix (original tupleValues v)",
      "original finPhysicalProfile (log Q) N (radialWidth h) (N^frozenLeafCutoffFloor) v != 0",
      "original labelledProduct v <= N",
      "Nat.Coprime (original labelledProduct v) N",
      "original NonradialSupport.properStrip at the unchanged physical log point"
    ],
    "role_of_complete_coprime": "Only the clean part of a positive partition. Its complement is paid at the original weighted noncoprimeSlice, which may include tail-divisor cases allowed by the original sharp source.",
    "source_condition_equivalence_claimed": false,
    "supported_floor_available_for_later_counting": true
  },
  "proof_chain": [
    "The existing logPoint_prefix_eq_log_product identifies the old physical logarithm with log((tuplePrefix u).prod); original fresh/tail projections give the remaining coordinates definitionally.",
    "The original bounded profile weight is in [0,1] by physicalProfile_bounds, including the zero prefix branch.",
    "Outside the inclusive inner product cap, or when the original weight vanishes, the comparison follows from nonnegativity.",
    "On complete-product noncoprimality, retain the same full original boundedProfileWeight in noncoprimeSlice.",
    "On the clean part, every fresh slot divides the complete product, so Nat.Coprime.of_dvd_left supplies only the needed original fresh-prime coprimality.",
    "Accepted geometry at the same physical log point gives every original raw gate through raw_iff_passed and the exact old-word log dictionary; the tuple lies in the existing sharpTuples.",
    "A nonzero clean inner tuple failing accepted geometry lies in original properStrip by the accepted inner_nonzero_failure_mem_properStrip theorem.",
    "Sum the pointwise unit-indicator bounds on the original finite prime carrier and identify those actual subset sums with their cardinalities.",
    "Sum over original Split admission, then apply sharp_count_le_ordinaryBad once to the combined sharp counts, preserving coefficient one."
  ],
